{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"vermicidal-combative-llama","creation_channel":"python","description":"## The defect\n\n`scripts/check_config_reads.py` (the 6754 gate, wired at `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:470`)\nis meant to fail CI when a `_config_schema.py` dataclass field has no read site unless it carries\na `# read-via:` marker. It does not do that.\n\n`_collect_reads` (script lines ~84-101) builds a GLOBAL SET of attribute NAMES read anywhere under\n`src/rebar`, then the check is effectively `field_name in reads` -- NOT `Class.field`.\n\nSo any field whose bare name is read on ANY other object anywhere in the tree passes the gate,\nregardless of whether that field itself is ever read.\n\n## Confirmed instance\n\n`CodeHealthConfig.enabled` (`src/rebar/_config_schema.py:538`) has ZERO read sites -- nothing\ngates on it, and structural metrics run even at the default `enabled=False`. It nevertheless\npasses the gate and carries no `# read-via:` marker, because `.enabled` is read elsewhere\n(`ui.enabled`, `LangfuseConfig`, and under `llm/`).\n\nContrast `CodeHealthConfig.analyzers` (:541), whose bare name is read NOWHERE in the tree, so it\nDID trip the gate and needed the marker `# read-via: inert pending bug dce2-b93d-4112-451c` at\n:540 to keep CI green.\n\nThat asymmetry is the tell: the gate catches an inert field only when its name happens to be\nglobally unique. That is luck, not a check.\n\n## Why this matters beyond one field\n\nThis is a FALSE-NEGATIVE class, not a single miss. Common field names -- `enabled`, `path`,\n`timeout`, `remote`, `branch`, `mode` -- are exactly the ones most likely to be reused across\nconfigs and therefore exactly the ones this gate cannot police. The inert-config family it exists\nto prevent (281f, 9d7a, dce2) is precisely the family it will keep missing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The gate resolves a read to the OWNING dataclass, not a bare attribute name -- a field is\n      satisfied only by a read of that class's instance, so a same-named attribute on an unrelated\n      config no longer satisfies it.\n- [ ] A RED-first test proves the current gate passes a synthetic inert field whose name is reused\n      elsewhere, and that the fixed gate fails it.\n- [ ] The gate is re-run across the whole existing `_config_schema.py` and every field it newly\n      flags is triaged -- each is either given a genuine read site, deleted, or given an accurate\n      `# read-via:` marker naming a real ticket.\n- [ ] `CodeHealthConfig.enabled` specifically is resolved as part of that sweep (see the sibling\n      bug), since it is the confirmed instance.\n- [ ] Any field whose marker points at a CLOSED ticket is reported rather than silently accepted.\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound by a code-trace archaeology pass over the metrics/code-health system while scoping\ndce2-b93d-4112-451c. The gate's own escape hatch for `analyzers` is self-referential -- the bug id\nin the marker is what keeps CI green -- which is legitimate, but it masked that the gate could not\nhave caught the neighbouring field at all.\n","id":"2c58-e710-275e-4ff7","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"The 6754 inert-config gate matches field names GLOBALLY, so a name reused by any other config hides an inert field"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785897851362932001,"uuid":"9e349cf8-046a-4500-89f1-de8c2851e45d"}